# Copy to `.env` beside compose.yaml and edit. `.env` is ignored by Git; this file is not, so nothing here is ever a
# secret. Every value below is a default the Compose file already applies, restated so the whole surface is visible in
# one place — an unset variable and the value shown here mean the same thing.
#
# Credentials do not live here. They are files under ./secrets/, mounted as Compose secrets, because a file can be
# permissioned and never appears in another process's environment block. Create these three before the first start:
#
#   mkdir -p secrets/mailfathom
#   chmod 700 secrets
#   chmod 711 secrets/mailfathom
#   chmod 755 config
#   openssl rand -base64 33 | tr -d '\n' > secrets/postgres-superuser-password
#   openssl rand -base64 33 | tr -d '\n' > secrets/mailfathom-database-password
#   chmod 444 secrets/postgres-superuser-password secrets/mailfathom-database-password
#
# A mailbox that authenticates with OAuth needs one file more: the data-encryption key its refresh token is sealed
# under. It belongs under ./secrets/mailfathom/, the directory MailFathom reads its own credentials from, rather than
# beside the two above, which are Compose secrets and arrive at /run/secrets instead:
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32 | tr -d '\n' > secrets/mailfathom/mailfathom-data-key
#   chmod 444 secrets/mailfathom/mailfathom-data-key
#
# That is `-base64 32`, never the `-base64 33` on the two lines above it. The material has to decode to exactly 32
# bytes, which is what AES-256 takes, and startup refuses any other length rather than accepting a weaker key — so the
# neighbouring command is the one thing never to copy here. Generate it once and back it up with the database: nothing
# in MailFathom regenerates it, and a database restored without its key restores nothing that was sealed. A deployment
# whose mailboxes all authenticate with a password needs no key and starts without one. See
# docs/operations/secret-provisioning.md for the whole contract.
#
# What is mounted has to be reachable by the container's own account; what is not mounted is what restricts access.
# MailFathom runs as an unprivileged account that is nobody's host user and holds no capability to override a mode with,
# and Compose bind-mounts with the host's own permissions rather than applying `mode`. ./secrets/mailfathom is a bind
# mount, so that account needs the execute bit on it — at 0700 every secret reference fails to resolve however the files
# inside are permissioned — while ./secrets is mounted nowhere and its 0700 is what keeps other users on the host out.
# The files are read rather than listed, so 0444 is right and 0600 reads to MailFathom as an unresolvable reference.
# ./config is mounted the same way and is listed rather than opened by name, so it needs read as well as execute (0755)
# and its *.json files need to be readable (0644). Git records no directory mode, so that one is set rather than assumed:
# a clone made under a strict umask leaves a directory the container cannot list, and a umask of 077 produces a
# deployment that does not start. See docs/operations/deployment-compose.md for the whole reasoning.

### The image
#
# Left at a name no registry can serve, so `docker compose up --build` builds this checkout and nothing is ever pulled
# by accident. To run a published release instead, name one at a specific immutable tag:
# `ghcr.io/krzysztof318/mailfathom:<version>`, or `docker.io/krzysztof318/mailfathom:<version>` for the mirror, which
# carries the same digest. Never a moving tag, and never a nightly identifier, which is unsupported development output
# and needs the deliberate opt-in in compose.nightly.yaml; ADR 0004 defines the channels and their tags.
# MAILFATHOM_IMAGE=mailfathom:local
#
# `build` keeps Compose from ever pulling `mailfathom:local` from the configured registry, where an unrelated image
# published under that unqualified name would silently replace this checkout. Change it to `missing` in the same edit
# that points the variable above at a published release image — the image and how it is obtained are one decision.
# MAILFATHOM_PULL_POLICY=build

### Where the container's ports are published
#
# Both pairs below are Compose's own publishing — the host address and the host port each container port is mapped to.
# Neither reaches MailFathom's configuration: every surface is told where it is served in its own section, which is why
# compose.yaml states McpEndpoint__Port in the environment block and why the host refuses ASPNETCORE_HTTP_PORTS and
# every other URL-shaped address rather than ignoring it. So each variable moves the outside of a mapping whose inside
# is fixed elsewhere.
#
# The MCP endpoint, published on loopback on purpose. MailFathom serves plain HTTP and terminates no TLS, so publishing
# it on an interface other than loopback exposes synchronized mail without transport protection. Change it only once a
# reverse proxy on the `frontend` network is what listens publicly. Inside the container the endpoint binds every
# address — which is what makes the mapping work at all, so this bind address is the whole of what restricts it.
# Moving the container-side port is `McpEndpoint:Port`, not a variable here, and the right-hand side of the mapping in
# compose.yaml moves with it.
# MAILFATHOM_HTTP_BIND=127.0.0.1
# MAILFATHOM_HTTP_PORT=8080
#
# The probes — `/started`, `/health`, and `/alive` — answer on a listener of their own, and never on the port above: a
# probe path asked on 8080 is answered with 404, and `/mcp` asked on this one is too. They carry no credential, so this
# bind address is the whole of their access control; keep it on loopback unless the machine asking is not this one.
# Moving the container-side port is `HealthEndpoints:Port`, not a variable here — see
# docs/operations/health-endpoints.md.
# MAILFATHOM_HEALTH_BIND=127.0.0.1
# MAILFATHOM_HEALTH_PORT=8081

### The database
#
# The role MailFathom connects as owns its database and is not a superuser; the initialization script creates it and
# installs the `vector` extension while a superuser still can.
# MAILFATHOM_DATABASE=mailfathom
# MAILFATHOM_DATABASE_ROLE=mailfathom
# MAILFATHOM_POSTGRES_IMAGE=pgvector/pgvector:0.8.6-pg18
#
# Naming a `-pg17` image here also changes where the data directory is: PostgreSQL 18 moved it into a version-specific
# subdirectory, and the Compose file mounts the volume at the parent that covers both. An older image would initialize
# an empty directory beside the data an 18 server wrote rather than reading it.
#
# The named volume holding the synchronized mail. `docker compose down` leaves it; only `down --volumes` destroys it,
# and rebuilding it costs a full IMAP resynchronization.
# MAILFATHOM_POSTGRES_VOLUME=mailfathom-postgres-data

### The client
#
# MailFathom's own client, served by the application as a page at the address the MCP mapping above publishes. Off,
# which is what makes it free: the bundle travels inside the image either way, so an install that wants only the server
# pays its size and nothing else — no route, no middleware, and nothing reachable.
#
# One variable, and it writes two settings: that the client is served, and that this deployment accepts the clear-text
# hop between the container and whatever terminates TLS in front of it. MailFathom refuses to serve the page over a
# clear-text socket without that second statement — the page, and every token a browser then sends back, would cross
# the network unprotected — and this container speaks plain HTTP by design, so enabling the client here is the
# statement that a reverse proxy exists. Do not enable it while MAILFATHOM_HTTP_BIND names anything but loopback and no
# proxy is listening.
#
# It needs `ClientEndpoint:Enabled` in ./config as well, since the page is served on that surface's listeners and
# calls them; enabling this without it is refused at startup, naming both. The reverse is not — an endpoint serving
# its routes with no page in front of them is what every deployment here does today.
# MAILFATHOM_CLIENT=false

### Personal-data scanning
#
# Off, which is the product's default and what makes it free: no analyzer container exists, no image is pulled, and none
# of its memory is held. Enabling it is **two** variables rather than one, because Compose cannot make an environment
# entry conditional on a profile — the profile decides whether the analyzer container exists, and the switch decides
# whether MailFathom asks it, so either one alone is a deployment that does not work:
#
#   COMPOSE_PROFILES=personal-data-scanning
#   MAILFATHOM_PERSONAL_DATA_SCANNING=true
#
# Enabling the switch without a reachable analyzer does not run unprotected: MailFathom starts, answers Unhealthy on
# /health, and refuses every read and derived write the scanner guards until the analyzer answers. Naming no address at
# all is the one case that still fails startup, and it names SensitiveContent__PersonalDataAnalyzer__Endpoint as the
# setting to correct. The first start after enabling it is slow — the analyzer loads a language model before it answers,
# and MailFathom reports unready for that interval rather than restarting.
# MAILFATHOM_PERSONAL_DATA_SCANNING=false
#
# Where MailFathom asks, defaulting to the analyzer this file starts. Point it at an analyzer you already operate and
# leave COMPOSE_PROFILES alone to use that one instead — but **keep it inside your own network**: the whole point of
# scanning is that content is inspected before it leaves the trust boundary, so an analyzer across the public internet
# hands the mail to a third party in order to establish whether it may be handed to one.
# MAILFATHOM_PERSONAL_DATA_ANALYZER=http://presidio-analyzer:3000
#
# The two-letter code of the first language the analyzer is asked in. A deployment may name several — a mailbox is mixed
# by nature, and a scan asks once per language and merges what came back — but a list reaches .NET configuration as
# indexed environment keys and Compose expands no variable into several of them, so this variable names the first and a
# second is the commented `SensitiveContent__PersonalDataAnalyzer__Languages__1` line in compose.yaml. Every language
# named has to be one the analyzer was built for: the pinned image carries an English model and an English recognizer
# registry, so naming another means supplying an analyzer built for it in the same edit, and MailFathom reports unready
# for as long as the analyzer recognises nothing in one of them rather than falling back to English. Each language is
# also another request inside the one budget a scan is allowed, and at most eight are accepted. Which identifiers each
# language reaches, and what building such an image takes, is in
# docs/operations/personal-data-analyzer-languages.md.
# MAILFATHOM_PERSONAL_DATA_LANGUAGE=en
#
# How sure the analyzer must be before a finding is redacted, from 0 to 1. Redaction acts on every finding whatever its
# confidence, so this floor is the only thing between the deployment and the analyzer's weakest guesses: with no floor an
# eight-digit build number is reported as a bank account number at 0.05 and a contract reference of a letter and seven
# digits as a driving licence at 0.3. The default is the one value that drops every measured false positive while leaving
# every category detectable, so raising it stops a passport number and a bank routing number from being found at all.
# MAILFATHOM_PERSONAL_DATA_MINIMUM_CONFIDENCE=0.4
#
# The analyzer image. An immutable tag, and the same pin the Helm chart and the integration suite use, so an analyzer
# reviewed against one is the analyzer running in the others. It is also where an image built for another language is
# named, since a model is installed while an analyzer image is built rather than while it starts.
# MAILFATHOM_PRESIDIO_IMAGE=ghcr.io/data-privacy-stack/presidio-analyzer:2.2.364

### Spam classification with a scanner
#
# Off, which is the product's default and what makes it free: no daemon container exists, no image is pulled, and none of
# its memory is held. Enabling it is **two** entries for the reason personal-data scanning is — Compose cannot make an
# environment entry conditional on a profile, so the profile decides whether the daemon exists and the switch decides
# whether MailFathom classifies and asks it:
#
#   COMPOSE_PROFILES=spam-scanning
#   MAILFATHOM_SPAM_SCANNING=true
#
# Enabling the switch without a reachable daemon does not classify quietly from headers alone: MailFathom refuses to
# start and names SpamClassification__Scanner__Host, the setting to correct. The first start after enabling it is slow —
# the daemon fetches its rule updates and compiles the corpus before it listens, and MailFathom restarts until it does.
#
# Classification **without** a scanner is the other half of the feature and needs neither entry: set
# `SpamClassification:Enabled` in ./config and mail is classified from the headers it already carries.
# MAILFATHOM_SPAM_SCANNING=false
#
# Where MailFathom scores, defaulting to the daemon this file starts. Point it at a daemon you already operate and leave
# COMPOSE_PROFILES alone to use that one instead — but **keep it inside your own network**: a scan sends the whole
# message, unredacted, to whatever answers there. A host name on its own, with no scheme and no path.
# MAILFATHOM_SPAM_SCANNER_HOST=spamassassin
# MAILFATHOM_SPAM_SCANNER_PORT=783
#
# **Whether the daemon resolves DNS blocklists, and it is off.** Those rules send the sending addresses and the URI host
# names out of the owner's mail to third-party lists, which is what scanning inside the trust boundary exists to avoid,
# and the free tiers answer a shared public resolver with a blocked marker rather than an answer. Off, the daemon runs
# local rules only. Set it to 1 if you operate a resolver of your own and accept what it sends.
# MAILFATHOM_SPAM_SCANNER_DNS_CHECKS=0
#
# What one scan may cost. The timeout is how long a message may take before it keeps the verdict its headers reached;
# the size limit is where SpamAssassin's own client truncates, which is the size its corpus was tuned against; the
# concurrency matches the number of children the daemon spawns by default, and sending more at once only queues them
# inside the daemon where this timeout cannot see the wait.
# MAILFATHOM_SPAM_SCAN_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30
# MAILFATHOM_SPAM_SCAN_MAXIMUM_MESSAGE_BYTES=512000
# MAILFATHOM_SPAM_SCAN_MAXIMUM_CONCURRENT=5
#
# The daemon image. A digest rather than a tag, because the upstream publishes no immutable version tag, and the same pin
# the Helm chart and the integration suite use.
# MAILFATHOM_SPAMASSASSIN_IMAGE=docker.io/axllent/spamassassin@sha256:9bea393891c92a3531cb7081e9b2c478a654a048c9a6fa6f9d1df4300bf3ab8b

### Bounds
#
# Logs are rotated so a long-running deployment cannot fill the disk with them.
# MAILFATHOM_LOG_MAX_SIZE=10m
# MAILFATHOM_LOG_MAX_FILES=3
#
# Resource limits. `deploy.resources.limits` is honored by `docker compose up` as well as by Swarm.
# MAILFATHOM_CPU_LIMIT=2
# MAILFATHOM_MEMORY_LIMIT=1g
# MAILFATHOM_POSTGRES_CPU_LIMIT=2
# MAILFATHOM_POSTGRES_MEMORY_LIMIT=1g
#
# The analyzer's, which are generous because the container holds a language model in memory for its whole life. Below
# roughly a gigabyte it is killed while loading, which reaches MailFathom as an analyzer that never became ready.
# MAILFATHOM_PRESIDIO_CPU_LIMIT=2
# MAILFATHOM_PRESIDIO_MEMORY_LIMIT=2g
#
# The spam daemon's, which are modest: it holds its compiled rule corpus in memory and forks a child per scan. Below
# roughly 256m it is killed while compiling rules, which reaches MailFathom as a scanner that never answered.
# MAILFATHOM_SPAMASSASSIN_CPU_LIMIT=1
# MAILFATHOM_SPAMASSASSIN_MEMORY_LIMIT=512m
